        <p>Understanding Spain's foreigner identification system</p>
      </div>
      <p>The <strong>NIE (Número de Identidad de Extranjero)</strong> is a unique identification number issued by Spain's tax authorities (Agencia Tributaria) to all foreign nationals. It serves as your tax identification number and is one of the most important documents you'll need when living, working, or conducting business in Spain.</p>
      <p>Your NIE is not just a number—it's your official identity within Spain's administrative system. It identifies you for tax purposes, bank accounts, property ownership, employment, business registration, and virtually every significant transaction you undertake.</p>

      <h3>How the NIE Format Works</h3>
      <p>Every NIE follows a specific format:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>First character:</strong> A letter (X, Y, or Z, depending on your passport/document type)</li>
        <li><strong>Seven digits:</strong> A sequential number assigned to you</li>
        <li><strong>Final letter:</strong> A letter calculated from the number (similar to a check digit)</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Example:</strong> X-1234567-Z. The first letter and final letter are always uppercase.</p>

      <h3>Key Facts About Your NIE</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Your NIE is assigned when you first request it and <strong>never changes</strong> throughout your entire time in Spain</li>
        <li>It is permanent—once issued, your number remains yours indefinitely</li>
        <li>You cannot "lose" your NIE; if you misplace the certificate, you can request a duplicate</li>
        <li>The NIE is required before you can open a bank account, buy property, or work legally in Spain</li>
        <li>It costs approximately €9.60 and is obtained from the National Police (Policía Nacional)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

