  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>Answers to common questions about NIE numbers</p>
      </div>
      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>What is an NIE number in Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>An NIE (Número de Identidad de Extranjero) is a unique identification number issued by Spain's tax authorities to foreign nationals. It serves as your tax identification number and is required for most activities in Spain, including opening bank accounts, buying property, and working. The number follows the format X-1234567-A, where X/Y/Z is a letter, followed by 7 digits and a final letter.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How do I get an NIE number in Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>To get an NIE number, you must: (1) Complete form EX-15 or fill out the online form, (2) Book a cita previa (appointment) at your local police station or National Police headquarters, (3) Gather required documents (passport, proof of address, completed forms), (4) Attend your appointment with originals and copies, (5) Pay the fee (currently around €9.60), (6) Receive your NIE number and certificate.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much does an NIE number cost?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>An NIE number is free to obtain from the Spanish tax authorities. However, there is a small fee of approximately €9.60 for the police administrative procedure. Form copies and notarization may incur additional costs if required. Overall, obtaining an NIE is one of the cheapest official procedures in Spain.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does it take to get an NIE number?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Once you attend your appointment, you typically receive your NIE number immediately on the same day. However, the challenge is booking the appointment itself—waiting times for a cita previa can range from 2 weeks to 3+ months depending on your location and demand. We recommend booking your appointment as early as possible, even before arriving in Spain.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need an NIE number to buy property in Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes, an NIE number is absolutely required to purchase property in Spain as a foreign national. You cannot complete the purchase process without one. It's recommended to obtain your NIE before beginning serious property negotiations. Some notaries can help with the process, but you must obtain the number from the police authorities.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is the difference between NIE and TIE?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>The NIE is a tax identification number (just a number, like 12345678-Z), while the TIE is a physical identity card. You receive your NIE upon request from the police. If you're granted residency in Spain, you'll also receive a TIE card, which is a physical document that serves as proof of your legal residence. Both use the same identification number, but the NIE is the number and the TIE is the card.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I get an NIE number online?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>You cannot apply entirely online, but you can begin the process online. You can fill out form EX-15 electronically and submit it, then book your appointment online through the National Police website (cita previa system). However, you must attend an in-person appointment to complete the process and receive your NIE certificate.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What happens if I lose my NIE number or certificate?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Your NIE number is permanent and stays the same throughout your life in Spain—it never changes. If you lose your NIE certificate, you can request a replacement from the National Police headquarters or your local police office. You don't need a new number; you just get a duplicate certificate of your existing NIE. The process is straightforward and typically completed quickly.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

